**Read:** [Fundamentals of Vacuum Technology — Walter Umrath and contributors](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Vacuum-Technology-Leybold.lingua#r_1S93A5X32978GSHZH4CF96VPQR), leak definitions and vacuum components; [Outgassing properties of vacuum materials — Paolo Chiggiato](../sources/Outgassing-Properties-Chiggiato.lingua#r_18MM2A316NWRJABS8E53468PWT), sources and accumulation; [Surface finishing and vacuum coatings — Mauro Taborelli](../sources/Surface-Finishing-and-Vacuum-Coatings-Taborelli.lingua#r_7KSQ0A3V3YH02RRXRKCRFAKRKD), cleaning and design access.

A real leak transfers matter across the intended enclosing boundary. A trapped pocket inside that boundary can release its finite inventory slowly through a narrow connection, producing a virtual leak. Blind holes, overlapping interfaces and incompletely vented cavities can create this behavior.

If a pocket of volume Vp is connected by constant molecular conductance C to a much lower-pressure chamber, its pressure decays as pp(t)=pp(0)exp(−Ct/Vp) in the fixed-temperature ideal model. The chamber sees a declining load. If the chamber pressure is not negligible, solve the coupled volumes instead.

**Work:** Derive the finite-pocket release and integrated amount. Compare its pressure trace with a constant external leak and with surface desorption. Explain why one fitted decay curve cannot always distinguish them. Identify a geometry or tracer observation that changes the evidence, including the response time of the measurement path.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
